<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andalusia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Andalusian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andalusia is a region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, whose name derives from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. Its cities, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, were centres of Islamic culture and power. It was a core territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>, whose rulers built monuments there, before it fell largely to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in the thirteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andalusia.jpg" alt="Andalusia" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miguillen This picture was made for the Taller de Heráldica y vexilología of Spanish Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Andaluc%C3%ADa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Andalusia occupies the south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, with the Atlantic and the Gulf of Cádiz to the west, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> to the east and south, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-gibraltar">Strait of Gibraltar</a> separating it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. In the north the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-morena">Sierra Morena</a> separates it from the plains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meseta-central">Meseta Central</a>. The valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a>, the longest river in Andalusia at 657 km, forms the Baetic Depression, and to the south lie the Baetic mountains, which include Mulhacén in the Sierra Nevada, the highest point of the Iberian Peninsula. The interior is the hottest part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in summer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The position of Andalusia at the southern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and Europe and between the Atlantic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, has made it a meeting place of civilisations since the Metal Ages. Its minerals and fertile land drew settlers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, who influenced early cultures such as Los Millares, El Argar and Tartessos. The region's history and culture have also been shaped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romanis</a> and Castilians.</p><p>The name comes from Arabic al-Andalus, which is first attested on dinars minted in 716 by the new Muslim government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, inscribed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Arabic. Its own origin is disputed. It was traditionally derived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>; later proposals include a Gothic term, put forward by Halm in 1989, and a pre-Roman source, suggested by Bossong in 2002.</p><p>During the Islamic Golden Age Córdoba surpassed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to become the biggest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and a centre of learning that produced many philosophers and scientists. The Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> settled the Guadalquivir Valley in the 13th century, and the mountainous east, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, was subdued in the late 15th century. Atlantic harbours then prospered on trade with the New World. An unequal distribution of land in large estates caused recurring unrest in the countryside in the 19th and 20th centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Andalusia is an autonomous community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, divided into eight provinces: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a>, Cádiz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huelva">Huelva</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaen">Jaén</a>, Málaga and Sevilla. These were set by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javier-de-burgos">Javier de Burgos</a> in the territorial division of 1833. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> is the capital, and the High Court of Justice sits in Granada. Many things regarded internationally as Spanish, among them flamenco, are Andalusian in origin. Authors of the region wrote in Arabic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> as well as Spanish, including Ibn Hazm, Ibn Tufail, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-zamrak">Ibn Zamrak</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> ibn Gabirol.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andalusia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The conquests of the thirteenth century transferred vast estates in <b>Andalusia</b> to the crown, the nobility, the Church and the military orders of Santiago, Calatrava and Alcántara, laying the foundations of a powerful landed aristocracy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…and other southern French lords, he took Zaragoza after a siege of about seven months in 1118, defeated an Almoravid army at the Battle of Cutanda in 1120 and in 1125–1126 led a raid deep into <b>Andalusia</b>, returning with thousands of Mozarab Christians to settle his new lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Alfonso VII recovered the offensive, campaigned into <b>Andalusia</b> and took Almería in 1147 with Genoese and Catalan help, though it was lost in 1157.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Abbasid government from its foundation, New Persian eventually rivalled Arabic as a language of administration and high culture across the eastern Islamic world, and Sasanian motifs such as the simurgh, the royal hunt and the enthroned audience scene spread through Islamic decorative arts from <b>Andalusia</b> to Central Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The bathing culture of <b>Andalusia</b> continues Romano-Visigothic practice; Reccopolis and Toledo show heavy Byzantine architectural influence, and the votive crowns of the treasure of Guarrazar, buried during the Muslim invasion, survive from treasures that amazed the conquerors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>They had close contact with the Greek colonies of Emporion, Rhode and Hemeroskopeion and with the Phoenicians of southern <b>Andalusia</b>, whose first colony in the peninsula, Gadir (Roman Gades, modern Cádiz), was founded in 1100 BCE, followed by Malaka, Sexi and Abdera.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
